      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "Wait, what? Llanowar Elves is 19/19? Ouroboroid 16/18? Breeding Pool p22 9/9? That's huge! Must be from counters multiplying? Gene Pollinator must be putting a lot of +1/+1 counters on creatures each turn.\n\nLet me check the board state: Llanowar Elves started as 1/1, now 19/19 (18 counters). Ouroboroid 0/0 base, now 16/18 (16 counters). Breeding Pool p22 just got 1 counter, but shows 9/9? That suggests it has 8 counters already? Or maybe the earthbend ability makes its base power/toughness equal to number of counters on it, then Gene Pollinator adds more counters.\n\nActually, Gene Pollinator says \"At the beginning of each end step, if a permanent you control was put into a graveyard from the battlefield this turn, put a +1/+1 counter on each creature you control and draw a card.\"\n\nI've been sacrificing creatures? Maybe Ouroboroid's ability: \"At the beginning of your upkeep, you may sacrifice another creature. If you do, return Ouroboroid to its owner's hand.\" I might have been sacrificing creatures each turn, triggering Gene Pollinator, adding counters to all creatures each turn.\n\nThat explains the huge stats. Also Breeding Pool p6 is 5/5 (showed earlier), but now p22 is 9/9. So Breeding Pool p22 must have gotten counters from Gene Pollinator triggers too.\n\nI should attack with all. But wait, opponent has Ral, Storm Conduit (9 loyalty). He can use his -2 ability to copy a spell, but that's not relevant for combat. He might have instant spells though.\n\nI'll attack with all creatures. Use attackers=[\"all\"].\n\n\n\n",
